<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">John the Baptist</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. c. 30</span> · <a href="/centuries/1st-century">1st century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">John the Baptist was a Jewish preacher active in the Jordan valley in the early 1st century CE, who called for repentance and baptised followers in the river, among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesus">Jesus</a>. Both the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gospels">Gospels</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/josephus">Josephus</a> record that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herod-antipas">Herod Antipas</a> imprisoned and executed him, the Gospels linking this to his criticism of Antipas's marriage to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herodias">Herodias</a>. He is honoured as a saint and prophet in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/john-the-baptist.jpg" alt="John the Baptist" width="320" height="475" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Titian · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Accademia_-_St_John_the_Baptist_by_Titian_Cat314.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>John is named in all four <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gospels">canonical Gospels</a>. Only the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gospel-of-luke">Gospel of Luke</a> tells of his infancy: he was the miraculous son of Zechariah, an old priest, and his wife Elizabeth, who was past menopause. The angel Gabriel is said to have announced his birth to Zechariah while he served in the temple at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a>. Luke also states that John and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesus">Jesus</a> were relatives, and on this account the Catholic, Anglican and Lutheran calendars place the feast of his nativity on 24 June, six months before <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christmas">Christmas</a>. Some scholars think that John belonged to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/essenes">Essenes</a>, a semi-ascetic Jewish sect that expected a messiah and practised ritual baptism.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>The Gospel of Mark introduces John as the fulfilment of a prophecy, a messenger sent ahead and a voice crying out in the wilderness. John wore clothes of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/camel">camel</a>'s hair and lived on locusts and wild honey. He proclaimed a baptism of repentance for the forgiveness of sin and said that another would come who would baptise not with water but with the Holy Spirit.</p><p>Baptism was the central symbol of his movement. The majority of biblical scholars agree that John baptised <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesus">Jesus</a>, and several <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-testament">New Testament</a> accounts report that some of Jesus's early followers had earlier followed John. The Gospel of Matthew has Jesus identify John as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elijah">Elijah</a> who is to come, while the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gospel-of-john">Gospel of John</a> has John deny that he is the Christ or Elijah.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>According to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-testament">New Testament</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herod-antipas">Herod Antipas</a> had John executed around 30 CE, after John rebuked him for divorcing his wife and unlawfully marrying <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herodias">Herodias</a>, the wife of his brother Herod Philip I. The Jewish historian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/josephus">Josephus</a> also mentions John in his Antiquities of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> and states that Herod Antipas had him executed at the fortress of Machaerus.</p><p>Followers of John persisted into the second century, and some proclaimed him the Messiah. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mandaeism">Mandaeans</a>, an ancient ethnoreligious group, regard him as their most important and final prophet. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> he is the prophet Yahya ibn Zakariya, and he is also honoured as a prophet in the Baháʼí Faith and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/druze">Druze</a> faith. In Catholic martyrology he is the only saint whose birth and death are both commemorated.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “John the Baptist” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Historians generally accept that he was born around 6–4 BCE, that he was baptised by <b>John the Baptist</b>, that he taught and gathered disciples in Galilee, proclaiming the coming kingdom of God, and that he was crucified in Jerusalem around 30 CE on the orders of the Roman…</blockquote><a href="/religion/christianity">Christianity · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>They revere <b>John the Baptist</b> and baptise repeatedly.</blockquote><a href="/religion/gnosticism">Gnosticism · Legacy</a></li><li><blockquote>It tells that Nasoraean Mandaeans, disciples of <b>John the Baptist</b>, left Jerusalem in the first century CE, reportedly because of persecution, and went to Haran or Hauran, then to the Median hills in Iran, and finally settled in southern Mesopotamia.</blockquote><a href="/religion/mandaeism">Mandaeism · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>They also hold that a &quot;Great Apostasy&quot; corrupted the early Christian church and that priesthood authority was restored through Smith, when angels including Peter, James, John and <b>John the Baptist</b> appeared to him.</blockquote><a href="/religion/mormonism">Mormonism · Beliefs &amp; Practice</a></li><li><blockquote>An inventory of 1476 shows the hanging displayed every year in Bayeux Cathedral during the week of the Feast of St <b>John the Baptist</b>, still the practice in 1728.</blockquote><a href="/work/bayeux-tapestry">Bayeux Tapestry · Reception</a></li><li><blockquote>In Luke's narrative Mary visits her cousin Elizabeth, who is pregnant with <b>John the Baptist</b>; the child moves in Elizabeth's womb, Elizabeth praises Mary for her faith, and Mary answers with the Magnificat.</blockquote><a href="/songs/magnificat">Magnificat</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Ein Karem, in what is now <a href="/countries/palestine">Palestine</a>. Died in Machaerus, in what is now <a href="/countries/jordan">Jordan</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=31.77,35.16&amp;year=20" aria-label="Ein Karem on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of John the Baptist"><image href="/maps/globes/g030n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5375 4840h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5375 4840h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=31.77,35.16&amp;year=20">What was here in 20 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
<section class="enc-words" id="in-their-words"><h2 class="enc-section-title">In their words</h2><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;O generation of vipers, who hath warned you to flee from the wrath to come?&#8221;</p><footer>Matthew 3:7, to the Pharisees and Sadducees who came to his baptism &#183; King James Version, 1611 &#183; as Matthew's Gospel records it &#183; <a href="https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/Bible_(King_James)/Matthew" rel="noopener">Wikisource</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;He that hath two coats, let him impart to him that hath none; and he that hath meat, let him do likewise.&#8221;</p><footer>Luke 3:11, answering the people who asked what they should do &#183; King James Version, 1611 &#183; as Luke's Gospel records it &#183; <a href="https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/Bible_(King_James)/Luke" rel="noopener">Wikisource</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;He must increase, but I must decrease.&#8221;</p><footer>John 3:30, of the one who was to come after him &#183; King James Version, 1611 &#183; as John's Gospel records it &#183; <a href="https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/Bible_(King_James)/John" rel="noopener">Wikisource</a></footer></blockquote><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/encyclopedia/quotations">Quotations from 595 people &rarr;</a></p></section>
